#module "tasks"
'' THE MODULE'S OWN GUARD: its headers tested 'defined( __FB_MT__ )', which is always
'' true -- __FB_MT__ is 0 or -1 -- so a build without -mt compiled and then crashed or hung.
#if __FB_MT__ = 0
    #error "std/tasks needs the multithreaded runtime: build with -mt"
#endif
'' std/tasks: scheduler -- tasks.Spawn, the goroutine scheduler
''
''     sub Worker( byval ctx as any ptr )
''         '' ...
''     end sub
''
''     tasks.Spawn( @Worker, @somecontext )
''
'' This is the library form of the 'spawn' STATEMENT.  It is public because the
'' statement has to be tested against something that already works, and because a
'' caller who has already built their own context has no reason to be forced
'' through the statement.
''
'' PREFER THE STATEMENT.  'tasks.Spawn( @Worker, @ctx )' puts the
'' entire lifetime of 'ctx' on the caller: it must outlive the goroutine, and
'' nothing checks that.  'spawn Worker( a, b )' evaluates and COPIES the
'' arguments at the spawn point, so the question does not arise.  A pointer to a
'' local is the classic way to get this wrong:
''
''     sub Bad( )
''         dim as long n = 5
''         tasks.Spawn( @Worker, @n )  '' n dies when Bad returns
''     end sub
''
'' A GOROUTINE RETURNS NOTHING, as in Go.  The channel is the return path.
''
'' THE SCHEDULER IS IN THE RUNTIME (src/src/rtlib/sched.c), not here.  It is a
'' process-wide singleton, and FreeBASIC could not make one from a header: a
'' 'dim shared' in a .bi included by two modules gave two of them, and there is
'' no lock available to guard a lazy first-use initialisation.  It also needs
'' fb_ErrorSetNum( ) for worker hygiene and a hook in fb_hRtExit( ) for
'' shutdown, neither of which is reachable from FreeBASIC.
''
'' WHAT YOU GET, stated plainly:
''
''   * Spawning is a queue push, not a thread creation.  Thousands of goroutines
''     are fine.  MILLIONS ARE NOT -- a goroutine blocked in a syscall holds an
''     OS thread, and the pool is capped at 256.
''   * The run queue is UNBOUNDED and Spawn never blocks.  A bounded queue would
''     dead-lock the moment it filled with goroutines that were each trying to
''     spawn.  Backpressure belongs on channels.
''   * Nothing joins goroutines.  At program exit, work still running is
''     ABANDONED -- silently.  Use a channel or a WaitGroup if it must finish.
''   * A goroutine runs on an arbitrary worker.  NEVER touch an HWND, a GDI
''     object or a COM apartment belonging to another thread from one; post the
''     result back through a channel the UI thread drains.
''   * A FreeBASIC runtime error inside a goroutine does not propagate.  The
''     worker's error state is reset between items so it cannot leak into the
''     next goroutine, but nobody observes it.  Send Result( of T, E ) instead.
''
'' REQUIRES -mt.


#if not defined( __FB_MT__ )
    #error "std/tasks requires -mt (the multithreaded runtime)"
#endif


'' ------------------------------------------------- the 'spawn' lowering half
''
'' These two exist so that the 'spawn' STATEMENT has
'' something to lower to; they are not meant to be written by hand.
''
'' WHY A VIRTUAL CALL RATHER THAN A TYPED THUNK.  The obvious lowering is a
'' synthesised thunk procedure per spawn site, taking the frame pointer.  It
'' cannot be written, because the frame type is a generic instantiation whose
'' name is mangled ($GEN$$...) and therefore not writable as text -- so the
'' thunk has to be generic too, and MEASURED: the address of a generic procedure
'' instantiation cannot be taken in FreeBASIC.  Every syntax fails:
''
''     dim as sub( byval p as any ptr ) f = @G( of long )
''         error 1: Argument count mismatch
''     ... = procptr( G( of long ) )
''         error 24: Invalid data types, before '('
''
'' So the address taken is of SpawnTrampoline, which is NOT generic, and the
'' typed part is reached through a virtual call on the frame.  One indirect call
'' per goroutine, which is nothing beside the queue push and the wake.
type SpawnWork extends Object
    declare abstract sub Invoke( )
    declare virtual destructor( )
end type

'' The destructor is VIRTUAL and that is load-bearing: the trampoline deletes
'' through a SpawnWork ptr, and a non-virtual destructor would skip the derived
'' frame's fields -- leaking every string, container and UDT passed to a
'' goroutine, silently.
declare sub SpawnTrampoline( byval p as any ptr )

'' FBCALL is __stdcall on 32-bit Windows and nothing everywhere else, so the
'' runtime exports _fb_Foo@N there and fb_Foo elsewhere.  An EXTERN "C" block
'' forces cdecl and fails to link on win32 ONLY -- and ALIAS does not rescue it,
'' because inside a NAMESPACE fbc mangles past the alias.  So match the
'' convention rather than fight it.
''
'' Found by rebuilding the win32 toolchain, not by any win64 test: win64 has one
'' calling convention and cannot tell the two apart.
#ifdef __FB_WIN32__
    #ifdef __FB_64BIT__
        extern "rtlib"
    #else
        extern "Windows"
    #endif
#else
    extern "C"
#endif

'' THE CALLBACK'S CONVENTION MUST MATCH THE PLATFORM'S DEFAULT FOR A PLAIN SUB,
'' and that default is not the same everywhere: a bare 'sub Worker( ... )' is
'' STDCALL on Windows and CDECL on Linux.  The enclosing extern block forces
'' cdecl on whatever it declares, so on Windows the parameter has to say
'' stdcall explicitly or every call site warns
'' "Calling convention mismatch in function pointer" -- and saying it
'' unconditionally produces exactly that warning on Linux instead.
''
'' Both halves were found by rebuilding a toolchain, not by a test: win64 sees
'' one convention and Linux sees the other.
#ifdef __FB_WIN32__
public declare sub Spawn alias "fb_Spawn" _
    ( byval work as sub stdcall( byval ctx as any ptr ), byval ctx as any ptr = nullptr )
#else
public declare sub Spawn alias "fb_Spawn" _
    ( byval work as sub cdecl( byval ctx as any ptr ), byval ctx as any ptr = nullptr )
#endif

'' Outstanding goroutines: queued plus running.  A snapshot, stale the instant
'' it returns.  Metrics only -- never control flow.
public declare function GoroutineCount alias "fb_GoroutineCount" ( ) as long

'' Worker threads currently in the pool.  Starts at the CPU count and grows
'' under sustained starvation, capped at 256.
public declare function WorkerCount alias "fb_SchedWorkerCount" ( ) as long

end extern

'' Definitions.  In this module rather than a .bas so that a program needs no
'' extra object to link -- the same shape the containers use.
destructor SpawnWork( )
end destructor

sub SpawnTrampoline( byval p as any ptr )
    if( p = nullptr ) then exit sub
    dim w as SpawnWork ptr = p
    w->Invoke( )
    delete w
end sub

